Android cross compilation
=========================

Android port was originally written by Ladislav Vaiz (OK1ZIA, 2012)
using a standalone Android.mk build system with a custom ltdl/dl
wrapper (since removed).  The present autotools-based cross-compile
instructions supersede that approach.

Hamlib can be cross-compiled for Android using the NDK's standalone
toolchains.  API level 28 is the minimum supported version; the
following example targets 64-bit ARM (aarch64) with API 28.

The authoritative unmodified file is from Hamlib tag 4.7.2, commit
40f63488fe0bd751b147f48d62fd217bf53713a0. It is retained because the
generated release archive omits this relevant build notice.

Prerequisites
-------------

Install the Android NDK (e.g. via Android Studio's SDK Manager or
`sdkmanager`). USB support (libusb) is not available in the Android cross
build, so `--without-libusb` is required. ShackCQ supplies an application-
owned external serial bridge instead of granting Hamlib direct USB access.

The upstream example and current ShackCQ build decision are recorded in
`docs/radio/hamlib/LICENCE_AND_PACKAGING.md` and
`docs/radio/hamlib/TRANSPORT_BRIDGE.md`.
